the first trip to hungary by a french  president in 14 years and at the prime  minister's office the visitor didn't shy  away from the big challenge that's  causing ongoing tensions in europe  i think that what europe has gone  through in recent weeks and we'll be  talking about this in particular with  our polish colleagues allows us to  reconsider a common organization  how to better prevent migrant flows and  protect our external borders and find  ways and means of a more efficient  cooperation between europeans on this  matter  victor orban's government is challenging  a ruling by the european court of  justice that hungary broke eu law by  deporting refugees over the border into  serbia last week the constitutional  court here ruled the government could  apply its own measures but sidestepped  the issue of whether eu law had primacy  over hungarian law that's an issue  that's already created a clash between  brussels and poland macron also joined a  meeting of the so-called visigrad group  the polish prime minister along with his  hungarian czech and slovak counterparts  earlier macron met leaders of the  opposition alliance that's challenging  orban in next year's elections  but despite clear concerns macron and  other leaders have about issues such as  lgbt rights in hungary and media freedom  there was agreement on what orban calls  strategic autonomy  in our opinion there is no autonomy  without a european defense industry or  without our own energy capability or  rather without nuclear energy and  there's no autonomy without  self-sufficiency in agriculture  both leaders have made it clear that  they don't see eye to eye on a variety  of issues but for president macron this  was really a chance to show the european  union that when his country takes over  the rotating presidency in january he'll  be able to handle internal disputes  no big breakthroughs then but a signal  the eu should be able to discuss the  toughest issues without acrimony how far  that's possible in the new year is far  from clear nadim baba al-jazeera  budapest
 

